2026-05-04 21:32:12 [@] currently focused on accepting and interacting with bots on port 8081
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] Looking at the code, the bot interaction on port 8081 is handled by the `slopprompt.ts` module
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] (exported functions: `listenService`, `slopBroadcast`, `announceCommand`) and consumed in
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] `relay.ts` via `slopPrompt` which races between stdin reads and network connections.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] Current Architecture
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 1. `listenService()` (in `slopprompt.ts`):
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - Starts a TCP listener on `localhost:8081` (configurable in `relay.ts` via `listen` config
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] flag).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - Calls `acceptConnections()`, which loops `listener.accept()` and for each new connection:
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - Stores it in `slopConnections` (Map).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - Starts a `readNamedConnection` promise (reads from the socket asynchronously).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - Stores the promise in `receivePromises` (object).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 2. `slopPrompt()` (in `slopprompt.ts`):
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - The main prompt loop races:
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - `readPromise` — a pending `reader.read()` from stdin.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - `timerPromise` — a 20ms timeout (interval parameter).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - `listenerPromise` (optional) — if listener is active.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]      - All `receivePromises` values (TCP read promises).
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - When a TCP read promise resolves, it parses the JSON (expects `{messages: [{message, from}]}`
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] or `{command: {name, args, from}}`) and stores the result in `response.messages` or
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] `response.line`.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - That response is then returned to `chat()` loop in `relay.ts`, where messages are pushed to
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] `rohaHistory` and optionally commands are executed.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 3. `slopBroadcast()` (in `slopprompt.ts`):
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - Writes NDJSON to all connected sockets. Currently iterates over `slopConnections`, writes JSON
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] like `{"messages":[{"message":"...","from":"..."}]}\n`.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4]    - Handles partial writes by looping.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] Potential Issues & Observations
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - Single-connection handling – `receivePromises` is an object (not Map) keyed by connection
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] name. When a read promise resolves, it’s deleted and a new one is started. That’s correct, but the
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] `readNamedConnection` function `setNoDelay` is not called.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - No connection heartbeats – If a bot disconnects ungracefully, the read promise may hang
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] indefinitely (until a timeout on the socket). The code in `slopBroadcast` removes connections that
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] cause write errors, but there’s no proactive cleanup for dead reads.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - JSON parsing fragility – `slopPrompt` parses the entire buffer as a single JSON object.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] If the bot sends multiple JSON objects concatenated (NDJSON), only the first is parsed; the rest is
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] lost. The current design assumes exactly one JSON per read. Better to use a line-based (or
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] newline-delimited) parser.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - No queue for incoming messages – The race model means one bot message is processed per
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] iteration of the prompt loop. If a bot sends many messages quickly, they might be interleaved or
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] starved.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - Error handling – `acceptConnections` runs an infinite loop but if `listener.accept()`
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] throws (e.g., too many open files), the loop exits and no further connections are accepted. The
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] listener promise in the race will also reject.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] - Security – No authentication; any local process can connect to port 8081.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] Suggestions for Improvement
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 1. Wrap sockets with a line-based reader – Use a `BufferedReader` (e.g., `Deno.BufReader`)
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] or implement a simple splitter that buffers partial reads and emits complete lines.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 2. Add connection health tracking – Use a timeout for read promises (e.g., `Promise.race`
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] with a timeout). If a socket hasn’t sent data in N seconds, close it and remove from
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] `receivePromises`.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 3. Queue incoming messages – Instead of returning to the prompt loop one at a time,
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] accumulate messages from all races and batch process them. Or at least use an array for pending
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] messages.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 4. Resilient acceptor loop – Catch errors in `acceptConnections` and re-enter the loop
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] (with a brief delay) to keep the port open.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 5. Clearer protocol – Document that the expected format is NDJSON with `{messages: [...]}`
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] or `{command: {...}}`. Ensure both sides agree.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 6. Optional authentication – Add a simple token handshake on connect (e.g., first message
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] includes an API key) for production use.
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] Would you like me to provide example code for any of these improvements, or do you have a specific
2026-05-04 21:32:32 [deepseek-v4] issue you're encountering with bot interaction?
