2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing the C++ source. This is an impressively integrated PAL video system for the
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] RP2350, combining composite video, chroma, teletext, I²S audio, and PS/2 keyboard in a single
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] project. I’ll highlight what is new or different, and flag a couple of potential issues.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] • What stands out*
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] - Chroma generation with PAL V‑flip – Your `initChroma()` precomputes two tables for even
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] and odd lines, intentionally swapping the 01 and 10 phase values on odd lines to implement PAL’s
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] alternating subcarrier phase. Many software PAL encoders skip this detail; including it is correct
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] and unusual.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext with full Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd parity – The pre‑computed lookup table
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] and packet construction for page header and body are thorough. The alignment with specific line
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] numbers (7‑22) is correct for PAL.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] - Combined DMA feeding multiple PIO state machines – The video, chroma, and teletext state
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] machines all run independently but synchronise via IRQs (`vidbit.pio` sets IRQ 4, chroma and
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] teletext wait for it). This allows the CPU to prepare frame data while the PIOs handle real‑time
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] timing.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] - Non‑standard system clock – 166.5 MHz is chosen to give exact PAL line timing (10656
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] cycles per line) and a convenient pixel clock. This is a clever trade‑off.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] • Potential bugs / areas to review*
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 1. DMA channel for chroma uses wrong PIO
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    In `chromadmainit()`, the DREQ is always taken from `pio0`:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio0, sm, true));
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    but in `main()` the chroma state machine is on `pio1`. This will cause the DMA to never trigger
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] or to read from the wrong FIFO. It should be `piogetdreq(pio, sm, true)` using the passed
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] `PIO` parameter.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 2. FDEBUG stall checks for chroma on pio0
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    The code checks `pio0->fdebug` for `chromaStall`, but the chroma state machine is on `pio1`.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] This will never flag a stall. A similar oversight exists for teletext (which is correctly on pio0).
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 3. Chroma DMA transfer count
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    The chroma PIO uses `FIFOJOINTX` and expects `colorWords` (120) words per field. That
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] matches your pre‑computed tables (120 entries). However, each word represents 2 subcarrier cycles,
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] so 120 words = 240 subcarrier cycles = ~13.5 µs. Is that enough for the active line? PAL active
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] line is ~52 µs, so you may need more words or a different loop in the PIO. The `chroma.pio` loop
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] sends words one‑by‑one via `out x,32` / `jmp !x start [16]` – it stops after one word? Actually it
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] reads 32 bits from FIFO into X, then jumps if X==0, else decrements X and sends 2 bits. That means
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] each FIFO word is a repeat counter for the 2‑bit output, not raw pixel data. The tables you fill
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] with 120 words of 32‑bit phase patterns are correct: each word contains 16 × 2‑bit phases, and X
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] holds the number of words? Wait – the PIO code does `out x,32` then `jmp !x start` – if X==0 it
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] restarts. But X is loaded from FIFO, then `jmp x-- loop` loops until X==0. That means each FIFO
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] word is not a counter but the actual data to output? Let's re‑read `chroma.pio`:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    start:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        irq clear 4
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        out x, 32          ; read (maybe) a word into X?
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        jmp !x start [16]  ; if X==0, restart
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    loop:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        out pins, 2 [1]    ; send 2 bits from OSR
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        jmp x-- loop       ; decrement X and repeat
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        set pins, 0
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    .wrap
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    Actually `out x,32` reads 32 bits from the FIFO into X (the counter). Then `out pins,2` sends 2
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] bits from the OSR (which is still containing the previous word). The OSR is never
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] refreshed! That means after the first word (30? actually 32 bits of OSR? wait shift register is 32
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] bits, out pins takes 2 bits at a time, after 16 outputs OSR is empty. But there is no `out isr` or
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] `pull` to refill. This is a critical bug: the `chroma.pio` never pulls new data from FIFO after
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] loading X. It will keep outputting the same 16×2 bits (the first OSR data at startup) indefinitely
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] while X decrements, then stop when X==0. This does not produce continuous chroma.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    I suspect you intended to use a pattern like:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    start:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        irq clear 4
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        out y, 32           ; number of words
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    loop:
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        out x, 32           ; load one data word into x? but you need to push to OSR
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]        out pins, 2 [1]     ; but OSR is empty
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4]    Actually the standard way to output a stream is to have a loop that `pull` (or `out null,32`?)
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] followed by 16 `out pins,2` with a counter. Your current code reads a counter into X, then
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] repeatedly outputs from OSR without refilling it. Please double‑check the PIO logic.
2026-05-07 17:29:14 [deepseek-v4] 
