2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] The `chroma.pio` program is clearly designed to work in tandem with `vidbit.pio` to add a
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] quadrature‑modulated color subcarrier for PAL. Several aspects deserve comment.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Clock divider and subcarrier rate
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - `clkdiv = 9.3885` (166.5 MHz ÷ 9.3885 ≈ 17.734 MHz) gives a PIO clock exactly 4× the PAL color
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] subcarrier frequency (4.43361875 MHz).
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - The loop emits one 2‑bit symbol every 4 PIO cycles (3 → `out pins,2 [2]` + 1 → `jmp x-- loop`).
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - Crucially: 4 such iterations would produce a full 360° cycle of the subcarrier (four 90°
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] steps) but would consume 16 PIO cycles, which is 4 subcarrier periods (16 ÷ 4). The
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] comment “4 bits per cycle” appears inconsistent:
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4]   - If a “bit” here means a 2‑bit symbol, then 4 symbols × 2 bits = 8 bits per subcarrier cycle.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4]   - The text says “8 bits from FIFO = 2 full color subcarrier cycles”, implying 4 bits per cycle,
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] which would represent only two phases (0° and 180°). That contradicts the intended 90° phase
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] resolution.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4]   - Recommendation: Clarify whether each subcarrier cycle should use 4 symbols (8 bits) or
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 2 symbols (4 bits). The hardware supports either, but the correlation with `vidbit`’s pixel counter
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] must match.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Synchronisation with vidbit
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - `wait 1 irq 4` waits for the IRQ set by `vidbit` at the start of the back porch. That timing is
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] correct for colour burst (which occupies the first ~10 subcarrier cycles of the back porch).
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - The subsequent `irq clear 4` is necessary to avoid double‑triggering.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - After the IRQ, `out x, 32` loads a 32‑bit word from the FIFO – presumably the number of 2‑bit
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] symbols to output for the current line (including burst and active video).
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Loop logic and zero‑length handling
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - `jmp !x start [16]` jumps to `start` (adding 16 delay cycles) if `x == 0`. This effectively skips
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] the line if no chroma data is supplied, while preserving a fixed timing offset. The 16‑cycle delay
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] may align the next `wait irq 4` to the correct phase relative to the line start.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - The main loop outputs 2‑bit symbols while decrementing `x`. After `x` reaches 0, `set pins, 0`
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] forces both DAC pins low, turning off the subcarrier output.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Output structure and DAC
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - The two‑pin DAC (00/01/10/11) drives a reconstruction filter (68 pF + 100 Ω/330 Ω). This can
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] produce four fixed amplitude levels, which is sufficient for a test pattern or colour burst, but
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] cannot represent the continuous‑amplitude, variable‑phase PAL chroma of a real video
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] signal.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - For full colour, a PWM‑based DAC or external DAC with more bits would be needed. The comment
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] implies this is a simplified demonstration.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Potential issues
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 1. Timing jitter: The `[16]` delay on the conditional jump adds uncertainty of ±16 cycles
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] (~0.9 µs at 17.7 MHz). This may be acceptable if the IRQ triggers at a known phase, but it could
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] shift the entire chroma waveform relative to the line timing.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 2. FIFO depth: The line counter and pixel counter in `vidbit` are loaded before the chroma
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] FIFO is accessed. The system must ensure that the chroma FIFO contains exactly the right number of
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] words per line, otherwise the two PIO programs will drift.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 3. IRQ collision: Both programs use `irq 4`. `vidbit` sets it; `chroma` clears it. No other
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] IRQ flags should be used in the same PIO instance to avoid conflicts.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 4. Fractional divider precision: 9.3885 cannot be represented exactly in the PIO’s 16.16
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] divider; the closest is 9 + 99/256 ≈ 9.3867, causing a tiny frequency error (~0.02%). Over a whole
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] field this may accumulate phase drift; for short bursts it is negligible.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Suggestions
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - Consider using a `mov` or `pull` with block (if the FIFO is empty) instead of the zero‑skip
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] logic, to let the PIO stall until data arrives.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - Align the comment about bits per subcarrier cycle with the actual implementation (4 symbols × 2
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] bits = 8 bits per cycle, thus 8 bits → 1 cycle).
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] - Document the relationship between the number loaded into `x` and the active video time: for PAL
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 576i with 864 pixels per line, the chroma datarate must be lower or equal to that of `vidbit`’s
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] pixel clock.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] Overall, the program is a compact and clever starting point for adding colour to the monochrome
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] sync generator. With a few corrections in the commentary and a clear definition of the data format,
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] it should work correctly.
2026-05-07 20:07:27 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing these files. The project is an ambitious RP2350‑based PAL video generator
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] combining composite sync/luma, colour subcarrier, teletext, audio, and PS/2 keyboard – all
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] synchronised via PIO state machines. I’ll comment on what is new or different and point out a few
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] technical points.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] Architectural highlights
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Multi‑PIO design: video and teletext on `pio0`, chroma and audio on `pio1`, keyboard on
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] `pio2`. This isolates timing‑critical streams.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Colour subcarrier via 2‑pin DAC: a simple 4‑level quadrature modulator using a pair of
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] GPIOs and a passive RC filter. This avoids external DACs and is a pragmatic trade‑off for
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] experimental colour.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Synchronisation between vidbit and chroma using `irq 4` – a clean way to align the colour
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] burst to the back porch.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext insertion with Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd parity – a faithful (if minimal)
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] implementation of the World System Teletext standard, inserted in the vertical blanking interval.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Continuous field update via DMA chaining (or immediate restart) – the main loop polls the
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] DMA completion and re‑triggers the next field without an explicit vsync interrupt. This is simple
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] but can work because the PIO stalls when its FIFO empties.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] PIO program observations
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - Timing for the front porch and back porch appears to have off‑by‑one errors in the constant
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] values (as noted in the earlier comment).
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4]   - Back porch: `set y, 28` gives 28 iterations (29 actual cycles) → 900 cycles, but the
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] comment says 964.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4]   - Front porch: `set y, 7` gives 7 iterations → 226 cycles, comment says 258.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4]   The sum of the components would then be slightly off the 10656 target; check the exact line
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] timing after correction.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - The pixel data is output via `out pindirs,1` – this toggles the pin direction rather than the pin
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] level. If the pin is configured as open‑drain (with a pull‑up), this can produce a composite
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] signal. More typical is to use `out pins,1` and drive the pin directly. The comment says “bitmap =
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] [27 x 309/310]” but the PIO reads 32‑bit words and outputs one bit per pixel. That implies only two
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] luminance levels (black/white). For grey‑scale, multiple bits per pixel would be needed.
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] `chroma.pio`
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] - The clock divider comment says `clkdiv=9.3885` (→ 17.734 MHz = 4‑× f_sc), but the C‑code uses
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] `2.3471f`, which yields ~70.96 MHz. This is a critical discrepancy: either the comment is
2026-05-07 20:10:56 [deepseek-v4] correct and the C code is wrong, or the program expects a different base clock.
