2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] We have examined the updated code. The critical change is in `chroma_init`:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] sm_config_set_out_shift(&config5, true, true, 2);
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] This enables autopull with a threshold of 2 bits, which fixes the earlier problem of the
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] OSR never refilling. However, the overall timing and data flow remain incorrect for PAL.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] Key issues
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 1. Counter value is far too large
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] In `initChroma`:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] int count = (colorWords-1)*16 - 1;  // (127*16) - 1 = 2031
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] This is loaded into `x` and decremented once per 2-bit sample. At the chroma PIO clock rate of
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] ~70.94 MHz (166.5 MHz / 2.3471), each sample takes 4 cycles ⇒ 2031 samples = 2031×4/(70.94×10⁶) ≈
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 114.5 µs.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] A PAL line is 64 µs. The active region (back porch + burst + video) is about 57.7 µs (≈
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 1020 samples at 17.735 MHz). So the current setting overflows into the next line’s sync, corrupting
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] the signal.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] • Correct value:* about 960 samples (40 burst + 920 active).
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] → Set `int count = 959;` (if 960 samples desired, decrement from 960 to 0 gives 960 iterations).
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 2. Burst length too short (32 instead of 40 samples)
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] `colorWord[2]` and `colorWord[3]` each contain 16 samples, giving 32 samples = 8 subcarrier cycles.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] PAL specifies 10±1 cycles (40±4 samples). Should increase to 40 samples (e.g., store burst in first
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 2.5 words, or adjust buffer layout).
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 3. DMA synchronisation is broken
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] - DMA is configured with `dmachannelconfigure(pio, sm, ... , NULL, UINT32_MAX, false)` —
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] infinite transfer.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] - The PIO program reads the first word as the counter (`out x,32`), then uses autopull to
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] read subsequent words for data.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] - Because the DMA loops endlessly through the ring buffer, after one line’s data is consumed, the
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] next FIFO word is not a fresh counter word but the next data word from the buffer.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]   For example, after processing 60 data words (960 samples), the PIO will try to start the next
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] line by reading the next FIFO word as a counter. That word will be part of the colour data (e.g.,
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] `0x23102310`), leading to a huge counter value and complete desync.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] • Solution:* Either:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] - Use a per-line DMA trigger (restart DMA each line with correct count and address), or
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] - Restructure the buffer so that the counter word appears at a fixed position and the PIO resets
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] its state (e.g., using `jmp !x start` correctly with the IRQ). Currently the program does `wait 1
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] irq 4` once at the start, but after the first line it never waits for IRQ again — it jumps
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] directly to `loop` because the counter will be reloaded from a garbage FIFO word.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 4. IRQ synchronisation is used only once
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] The program:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] start:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     wait 1 irq 4
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     irq clear 4
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     out x, 32
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     jmp !x start [16]
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] loop:
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     out pins, 2 [2]
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     jmp x-- loop
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4]     set pins, 0
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] After `jmp !x start`, if x is non‑zero it goes to `loop`. When the counter reaches zero, `jmp x--`
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] falls through to `set pins,0` and then `.wrap` jumps back to `start`.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] But on wrap, the PIO does not wait for IRQ again (the `wait` instruction is at `start`).
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] The `.wrap` goes to `start` immediately, so the second line will read the next FIFO word as counter
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] without waiting for the video state machine to assert IRQ 4.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] Furthermore, the `jmp !x start` is never executed after the first line because after `.wrap` the
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] program runs `start` which does `wait 1 irq 4` again? Actually the `.wrap` goes to the label
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] `start`. Yes, after `.wrap` it jumps back to `start`. So the program does wait for IRQ 4 on
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] every line. That is correct. The problem is that the DMA continues filling the FIFO while the PIO
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] is waiting, so when it finally waits and reads the counter, the FIFO may already contain leftover
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] data from the previous line.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] Because the DMA runs continuously, the FIFO will be prefilled with data words. When the PIO waits
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] for IRQ 4, the FIFO already has words waiting. The first word it reads (`out x,32`) will be the
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] next word in the FIFO after the previous line’s data. That word is likely a data word, not
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] the intended counter. So every subsequent line gets a wrong counter.
2026-05-07 20:45:55 [deepseek-v4] 
