2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] New Content: `colorfifo.pio` and `colorpump.pio`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] These two PIO programs implement a color pixel generation scheme where:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - `colorpump` reads a 2-bit pixel value from the FIFO, outputs it to two pins (the color
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] index), and then pulses a corresponding one of four side‑set pins low (active‑low) to trigger the
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] appropriate color generator.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - `colorfifo` (one instance per color index) waits for its trigger pin to go low, reads the
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 2-bit index from shared input pins (which reflect the current index), uses that index to select one
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] of four pre‑loaded RX FIFO entries, then outputs a 6‑cycle pulse train on three pins (presumably R,
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] G, B or Y, Pb, Pr) and releases.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] This is a clever approach to generating multi‑bit color on a PIO that only has a small number of
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] pins, by distributing the color generation across multiple state machines. However, there are
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] several points to examine:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 1. `colorfifo.pio` – Use of `rxfifo[y]` with `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The `mov` instruction in PIO can take `rxfifo[y]` as a source – meaning you can index the RX FIFO
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] using the lower bits of a register? Actually the PIO instruction set does not support
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] `rxfifo[y]` as an addressing mode. The only allowed sources/destinations for `mov` are `pins`, `x`,
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] `y`, `null`, `status`, `isr`, `osr`, `pc`, `exec`, and `out`/`in` shifts. The notation `rxfifo[y]`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] is not valid.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - Even if you intend to read from the RX FIFO directly, you cannot index it by `y`. The FIFO is a
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] queue; you can only read the front via `mov osr, rxfifo` (no index). To select among four different
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] values, you would need four different state machines, or use the ISR/OSR as lookup tables, or use
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] DMA to fill a scratch memory and use OUT with autopull.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The `.fifo txget` directive is not a standard PIO assembler keyword. Perhaps it’s a custom
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] comment or intended to indicate FIFO join, but it should be removed or replaced with a proper
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] comment.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 2. `colorpump.pio` – Side‑set pin selection
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The `set pins, 14` (binary 1110) clears bit 0, so only pin 0 goes low. Similarly `13` (1101)
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] clears pin1, `11` (1011) clears pin2, `7` (0111) clears pin3. That’s a valid way to pulse one of
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] four trigger lines low. The subsequent `set pins, 15` sets all four high again (all inactive).
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] Good.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - However, after `set pins, 14` there is a `jmp done` which will take 1 cycle; then `done:` has
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] `nop [1]` and `set pins, 15`. This means the low pulse lasts 2 cycles (one for set, one for jmp,
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] then nop delay? Actually the jmp is unconditional so it jumps to done; the nop spends 1+delay=2
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] cycles. That gives total low time of about 12 ns at 166.5 MHz, which is very short. Might be okay
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] for an external latch, but verify timing.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The `out x, 2` after `out pins, 2` re‑loads x with the same 2-bit value? Actually `out pins, 2`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] shifts 2 bits from OSR to pins. Then `out x, 2` shifts another 2 bits from OSR into x, but the last
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 2 bits of the FIFO word might be different. This seems like a bug unless the FIFO word contains 4
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] bits (low 2 = pixel, high 2 = copy for index? But the pixel value should just be 2 bits; after `out
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] pins,2` the OSR advances by 2 bits, and `out x,2` reads the next 2 bits which are undefined (could
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] be zero if autopull refills with word). It would be simpler to use `mov x, pins` after the output,
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] or use side‑set to drive the trigger lines directly without a separate x load.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 3. Synchronization between `colorpump` and `colorfifo`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The colorfifo machines wait on `jmp pin stall` – they spin until the trigger pin (one of four)
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] goes low. The pin used is the GPIO associated with that state machine’s `set` pin mapping? Actually
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] `jmp pin` tests a single input pin (by default pin 0 of the SM’s input mapping). To test a
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] different pin per state machine, each colorfifo should have its own input pin mapping. However, the
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] documentation says “reads pins[0..1] index” – that suggests two pins are read for the index, and
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] the trigger is a separate pin. The program uses `jmp pin stall` – which pin? The comment says “jmp
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] pin 2” – perhaps pin 2 is the trigger? But PIO’s `jmp pin` uses the pin defined by `JMP_PIN` in the
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] configuration, not a fixed pin number. It would be better to use `wait 0 pin, 0` or `wait 1 irq` to
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] synchronize.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The index pins (0..1) are shared among all colorfifo machines; each machine reads them after
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] being triggered. That works if the index is stable when the trigger fires.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 4. PIO program memory and clock domain
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - You are likely running these colorfifo machines at a higher clock than the video state machine?
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] The video pixel clock might be 13–14 MHz (PAL), but these PIOs can run at 166.5 MHz. The 6‑cycle
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] output pulse train at 166.5 MHz lasts about 36 ns, which is very fast for driving external DACs or
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] filters. It might cause synchronization issues with the video luma. Also, the `set pindirs, 0` at
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] the end disables the outputs, causing them to float – similar issue as in `vidbit.pio`.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 5. Overall architecture
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The concept of using one PIO state machine per color value (here four) is viable. But you could
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] instead use a single state machine with a lookup table in OSR/ISR memory or using `out x, 2` and a
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] compute block. Given the RP2350’s extra PIO resources, spreading across machines is fine.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] Recommendations
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 1. Replace `mov osr, rxfifo[y]` with a mechanism that loads the appropriate pulse‑train
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] data. Options:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4]    - Pre‑load the four pulse‑train words into the RX FIFO of each colorfifo machine before starting
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] (they are already loaded via DMA). Then simply `mov osr, rxfifo` (no index) to get the next word.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] Since each machine is triggered for a specific color, it only needs one wave pattern. If you want
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] different patterns per pixel, you need to re‑load the FIFO per pixel – which is complex.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4]    - Use the OSR as a lookup table: store the four 3‑bit patterns in scratch memory (ISR/OSR) and
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] use `out` with indexing – but PIO lacks indexed OUT. You could use `out isr, 2` then `mov osr, isr`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] with rotation? That’s messy.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 2. Ensure proper synchronization:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4]    - Let `colorpump` set a GPIO high after outputting the pixel index, and have each `colorfifo`
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] wait on a rising edge on its dedicated pin using `wait 1 pin, 0` (or `wait 0` for falling edge).
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] The `wait` instruction is more reliable than `jmp pin` (which polls and may consume 2 cycles per
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] iteration).
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 3. Double‑check pin assignments:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4]    - `colorpump` outputs 2 pixel index bits to pins (e.g., GPIO0, GPIO1). It also uses side‑set
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] pins (GPIO2..5?) to pulse four trigger lines. Each `colorfifo` machine monitors one trigger line as
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] its input pin (via `JMPPIN` or `WAITPIN`), and reads the shared index pins as its
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] `IN`/`MOV` source.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 4. Avoid floating outputs: Replace `set pindirs, 0` with `set pins, 0` and keep pindirs
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] always output. The pulse train should be output as pin values, not pin directions.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 5. Comment the `.fifo` directive – it’s not standard; use `; FIFO joined` as a comment.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] Conclusion
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] The new files introduce an interesting distributed color generation method. The main technical
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] issues are the invalid `rxfifo[y]` indexing and the potential synchronization and timing pitfalls.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] If you intended to use four independent state machines each serving one fixed color, the simpler
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] approach is:
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - Pre‑load each machine’s RX FIFO with the 6‑cycle pulse train (18 bits) as a 32‑bit word.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - Trigger the machine via `wait 0 pin` (or IRQ).
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] - The machine does `out pindirs, 3` six times, then `set pindirs, 0` (or `set pins, 0` if using pin
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] values).
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] This avoids the need for an indexed FIFO read.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] If you have a specific design goal (e.g., per‑pixel color selection from a palette), please
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] clarify, and I can suggest a more robust implementation.
2026-05-11 19:56:23 [deepseek-v4] 
