2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] For a first test, a master palette that makes each palette+index combination distinguishable on a
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] scope — encode the colour number itself into the pulse pattern:
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] ColorEntry makeTestEntry(int colour) {
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// colour 0..15: low nibble pattern visible as Y/Pb/Pr drive
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	ColorEntry e = {{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }};
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[0] = colour & 0x7;	// first 3 cycles encode low 3 bits
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[1] = colour & 0x7;
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[2] = colour & 0x7;
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[3] = (colour >> 3) & 0x1;	// last 3 encode bit 3 on Y
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[4] = (colour >> 3) & 0x1;
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	e.cycle[5] = (colour >> 3) & 0x1;
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	return e;
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] }
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] void buildTestMasterPalette(MasterPalette &out) {
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	for (int c = 0; c < TOTAL_COLORS; c++) {
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 		out[c] = makeTestEntry(c);
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 	}
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] }
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] A couple of design questions worth pinning down before silicon:
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] • Palette ordering convention — does palette index 0 correspond to colorpump pulsing
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] the first* wait pin (GP42) or the last? colorpump.pio's branch sequence `t3 → t2 → t1 → t0`
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] after `out x, 2` means `x=0` falls through to t0 (set pins 14 = bit 0 low → GP42 if colorpump's
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] `set` base is GP42). So palette 0 = SM0 = GP42 wait pin. Worth confirming the colorpump's set/out
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] pin bases match this assumption before wiring up.
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] • Index pin order* — the colorpump's `out pins, 2` writes the low 2 bits of its output FIFO
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] word to its `out` pins (the shared index pins GP46/47). The colorfifo's `mov y, pins` reads its
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] `in` base (GP46/47). If the bases align, `y` directly equals the index — no swizzle needed. If
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] they're misaligned by one pin, the low bit and high bit get swapped and the palette lookup mirrors.
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] Easy to fix in software but cleaner to nail the GPIO mapping.
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] • Pre-enable race — by enabling all four SMs after loading, the FIFOs are populated
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] before the first wait pin can be pulsed. Good. But the wait pins must be pulled high before*
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] the SMs enable, otherwise an SM may immediately fall through `jmp pin stall` on enable and execute
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] a phantom burst with `y` reading whatever the index pins happen to be. The `gpiopullup` in
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] `configureColorfifoPins` covers this provided colorpump hasn't started yet.
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] Want me to draft the colorpump-side setup next, or do you want to bring up a single colorfifo SM in
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] isolation first (manual wait-pin pulse from CPU) to confirm the palette pulse shape on a scope?
2026-05-11 20:02:36 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:04:34 [simon@midnightblue.local] i like to think of the bit trains as lsb first as though they trvalling from left to right on the page
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] Makes sense — and the PIO is on your side here: the default OSR shift direction is right, so
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] cycle[0] (lowest bits in the packed word) comes out first in time. The encoding I had already maps
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] "earliest in time" to "lowest bit address" which matches "leftmost on the page". But the C binary
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] literals like `0b001` break the convention — they're MSB-on-the-left.
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] Two changes to make the source read left-to-right:
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] • 1. Use named channel bits instead of binary literals* so the reader doesn't have to
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] decode bit positions:
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] constexpr uint8_t Y_	= 1 << 0;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] constexpr uint8_t PB	= 1 << 1;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] constexpr uint8_t PR	= 1 << 2;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] constexpr uint8_t __	= 0;	// idle slot, reads as a gap
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] const ColorEntry testPalette[TOTAL_COLORS] = {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{{ __,    __,    __,    __,    __,    __    }},	// 0: idle
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{{ Y_,    Y_,    Y_,    __,    __,    __    }},	// 1: Y pulse front
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{{ __,    __,    __,    PB,    PB,    PB    }},	// 2: Pb pulse back
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{{ Y_|PB, Y_|PB, __,    __,    PR,    PR    }},	// 3: mixed
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// ...
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] };
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] Each row now reads as a literal waveform across time, channels OR'd at each cycle.
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] • 2. If you want a more diagrammatic form*, encode per channel as a row, where column =
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] cycle, and pack at build time:
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] struct ColorPattern {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	const char *y;		// e.g. "111000" — left char is cycle 0
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	const char *pb;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	const char *pr;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] };
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] uint8_t encodeBit(char c) {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	return (c == '1') ? 1 : 0;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] }
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] ColorEntry buildEntry(const ColorPattern &p) {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	ColorEntry e = {};
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	for (int i = 0; i < PALETTE_CYCLES; i++) {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 		e.cycle[i] =	(encodeBit(p.y[i])  << 0) |
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 				(encodeBit(p.pb[i]) << 1) |
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 				(encodeBit(p.pr[i]) << 2);
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	}
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	return e;
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] }
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] const ColorPattern testPatterns[TOTAL_COLORS] = {
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// 0: idle	  Y: ......   Pb: ......   Pr: ......
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{ "000000", "000000", "000000" },
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// 1: Y burst	  Y: 111000   Pb: ......   Pr: ......
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{ "111000", "000000", "000000" },
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// 2: Pb burst
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{ "000000", "111000", "000000" },
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// 3: Pr burst
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	{ "000000", "000000", "111000" },
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// ...
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] };
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] The second form is more verbose to type but the source reads exactly like a logic-analyser
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] screenshot — time across, channels stacked. Worth it for a 16-colour table you'll be tweaking by
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] ear/eye.
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] Either flavour, the underlying packed word and the PIO behaviour are identical. Which feels closer
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] to how you want to author the palette?
2026-05-11 20:05:05 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:06:57 [simon@midnightblue.local] what is binary encoding in c++ look like for uint32_t colors[]={0b0011001100111110000,
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] C++14 onwards supports the `0b` prefix for binary literals directly, and you can use `'` as a digit
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] separator (C++14) to group bits visually:
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] uint32_t colors[] = {
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 	0b00'110'011'001'111'10000,
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 	0b00'000'000'000'000'00000,
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 	// ...
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] };
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] A few things worth knowing:
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] • Type and width* — `0b...` literals are typed as `int` by default (or `unsigned int` if
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] too large for signed). For a `uint32_t` initialiser the compiler will convert, but for explicitness
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] in tables you can suffix:
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000u	// unsigned
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000ul	// unsigned long
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 0b0011'0011'0011'1110'0000U	// same as u
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] • Width is not implied by leading zeros* — `0b0011` and `0b11` are the same value (3). The
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] visible bit-width is purely cosmetic; only the stored value matters. So if you want a packed 18-bit
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] colorfifo word in a 32-bit slot, write the full 18 bits with leading zeros for readability but
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] understand the top 14 bits of the uint32 are still zero.
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] • Separator placement is free* — `'` can go anywhere between digits and doesn't affect
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] value:
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 0b000'111'000'111'000'111	// group by 3 (per cycle, 3 channels)
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 0b111000'111000'111000	// or by per-cycle triplets the other way
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] For your colorfifo palette, grouping by 3 (one group = one cycle of Y/Pb/Pr) reads naturally
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] right-to-left in time since bit 0 (rightmost in the literal) comes out of the OSR first. If
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] you want left-to-right-in-time in source, you'd write the bits reversed — which fights against how
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] everyone reads binary numbers. Probably worth keeping binary literals as "rightmost cycle is
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] earliest" and just commenting accordingly, or using the array-of-cycle-bytes form from before where
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] source order = time order naturally.
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] • MSVC quirk* — older MSVC needed `/std:c++14` or later; pico-sdk's GCC has supported `0b`
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] since forever.
2026-05-11 20:07:10 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:37:10 [simon@midnightblue.local] colorpump_init is work in progress
2026-05-11 20:37:29 [claude-opus-4-7] Noted on the shared files. A few observations on what stands out as new/different compared to
2026-05-11 20:37:29 [claude-opus-4-7] typical patterns:
2026-05-11 20:37:29 [claude-opus-4-7] 
2026-05-11 20:37:29 [claude-opus-4-7] • colorpump architecture* — the split between `colorpump.pio` (2-bit index emitter +
2026-05-11 20:37:30 [claude-opus-4-7] side-pulse trigger) and `colorfifo.pio` (4 slave SMs gated by `jmp pin`, each holding a preloaded
2026-05-11 20:37:30 [claude-opus-4-7] 6-cycle pindirs train in RX FIFO) is an interesting design. Using `rxfifo[y]` as an indexed lookup
