2026-05-11 20:44:13 [simon@midnightblue.local] the palette will be unique 16 colors in due course and be static
2026-05-11 20:44:46 [simon@midnightblue.local] how is that?
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is actually a brilliant way to leverage the RP2350's new indexed FIFO reads. You are
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] effectively building a 16-entry lookup table by concatenating the 4 FIFOs across the 4 state
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] machines.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is exactly how your architecture achieves this:
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. The 4-Bit Color Split (The Pump)*
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you feed a 4-bit color index (0 to 15) into the `colorpump` SM, it splits that nibble in half:
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `out pins, 2` takes the lower 2 bits and puts them on the shared `COLORI0` and `COLORI1`
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] pins.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `out x, 2` takes the upper 2 bits and puts them in the `X` scratch register.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. The Trigger Routing*
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The `colorpump` then uses those upper 2 bits (`X`) to decide which of the 4 trigger pins to pulse
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] low:
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If X=0 (Colors 0-3): `set pins, 14` (Binary `1110`) -> Pulls GP2 low.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If X=1 (Colors 4-7): `set pins, 13` (Binary `1101`) -> Pulls GP3 low.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If X=2 (Colors 8-11): `set pins, 11` (Binary `1011`) -> Pulls GP4 low.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If X=3 (Colors 12-15): `set pins, 7`  (Binary `0111`) -> Pulls GP5 low.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. The FIFO Lookup (The Fifos)*
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because each of the four `colorfifo` SMs is configured to wait on a different trigger pin (`jmp pin
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] stall`), only one SM wakes up.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • That awakened SM reads the lower 2 bits from the shared pins (`mov y, pins`).
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • It executes `mov osr, rxfifo[y]`.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Because it's configured with `.fifo txget`, the FIFO acts like a static 4-word RAM. It grabs the
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] specific pulse train for that color, shifts it out to the video pins, and goes back to sleep.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to set this up in C++
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] To make this static 16-color palette work, your initialization routine just needs to preload the 16
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse trains into the 4 SMs before you enable them.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] You would do this right inside your `colorpump_init` (or `initColorfifos`) function. Conceptually,
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] it looks like this:
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Assuming color_trains[16] holds your 18-bit pindirs sequences
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] int color_index = 0;
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] for (int sm = 0; sm < 4; sm++) {
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// 1. Configure the SM (pin mappings, etc.)
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// ...
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// 2. Preload the 4 colors for this specific SM
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		pio_sm_put_blocking(pio2, sm, color_trains[color_index]);
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		color_index++;
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// 3. Enable the SM
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio2, sm, true);
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once you push those 4 words into each SM's TX FIFO, they sit there permanently. The `txget` mode
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] ensures they are not consumed when read by `rxfifo[y]`.
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • One crucial detail for your SM setup:*
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] When configuring the 4 `colorfifo` SMs, you need to assign a different `jmp` pin to each one so
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] they listen to the correct trigger from the pump:
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM0 config needs `smconfigsetjmppin(&config, PUMP0_ENABLE);`
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM1 config needs `smconfigsetjmppin(&config, PUMP1_ENABLE);`
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM2 config needs `smconfigsetjmppin(&config, PUMP2_ENABLE);`
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM3 config needs `smconfigsetjmppin(&config, PUMP3_ENABLE);`
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Does this align with how you were visualizing the 16-color mapping?
2026-05-11 20:46:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are definitely on the right track with the nested loops for preloading the 16 colors, but there
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] are a few copy-paste artifacts and initialization order issues in `colorpump_init` that will
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] prevent it from compiling and running correctly.
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a breakdown of what needs adjusting:
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Initialization Order: You are calling `piosmputblocking` and
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `piosmsetenabled` before `piosminit`. The state machine must be
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] initialized with its config before you can push data into its FIFO or enable it.
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Config Generators: Both SMs are still calling
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `wstprogramgetdefaultconfig`. They need to call their respective generated
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] functions (`colorpumpprogramgetdefaultconfig` and
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `colorfifoprogramgetdefaultconfig`).
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Pin Mappings:
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * `colorpump` needs its `out` pins mapped to `COLORI0` (for the 2-bit index) and its `set`
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] pins mapped to `PUMP0ENABLE` (for the 4 trigger lines).
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro]    * `colorfifo` needs its `jmp` pin mapped to the specific `PUMPENABLE` line for that SM. It
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] needs its `in` pins mapped to `COLORI0`, and its `out`/`set` pins mapped to `Y_PIN` (so `out
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] pindirs, 3` targets the component video pins).
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Variable Shadowing: Inside the `for(int sm=0; sm<4; sm++)` loop, the `pioStatus` and
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] `offset` variables have typos referencing outer scopes.
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the corrected `colorpump_init` function with the proper pin mappings and initialization
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] order:
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:07:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
