2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The cleanup looks great. The duplicate functions are gone and the structure is solid.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, looking closely at the PIO assignments, there is a classic RP-series hardware gotcha
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] hiding in your pin initialization that will prevent the video pins from outputting anything.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] In `main()`, you are passing `pio1` into `colorpump_init(pio1, ColorPumpStateMachine)`.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Inside the function, you initialize the video pins to `pio` (`pio1`):
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Initialize the video output pins
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, Y_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, PB_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio, PR_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] But your `colorfifo` state machines, which actually execute the `out pindirs, 3` and `set pindirs,
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 0` instructions, are hardcoded to run on `pio2`.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because a GPIO pin can only be controlled by one hardware block at a time, you must assign the `Y`,
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] `PB`, and `PR` pins to `pio2`. (Inputs are fine—`pio2` can read the pump pins even if they belong
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] to `pio1`, but outputs/pindirs strictly follow the GPIO multiplexer).
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Change those first few lines to:
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Initialize the video output pins to pio2 (the colorfifo block)
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio2, Y_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio2, PB_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio_gpio_init(pio2, PR_PIN);
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] One minor detail to double-check: Shift Direction
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `color_trains` are 18-bit values:
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0b0'000'000'000'000'000'0'111'110'011'001'111`
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since we didn't explicitly call `smconfigsetoutshift(&config7, ...)`, it defaults
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] to shift right. This means your `out pindirs, 3` will consume the lowest 3 bits
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] first (`111`), then the next 3 (`001`), and so on, moving right-to-left through your binary
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] literal.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you intended for the bits to be consumed left-to-right (highest bits first), you'll just need to
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] add:
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] `smconfigsetoutshift(&config7, false, false, 32);` inside the FIFO loop.
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Other than the pin muxing to `pio2`, the logic and architecture look ready to test on the silicon!
2026-05-11 21:29:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] The updates look spot on. You've successfully removed the duplicate function and correctly mapped
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] the `YPIN`, `PBPIN`, and `PR_PIN` to `pio2` so the `colorfifo` state machines can actually
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] drive them.
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] I also noticed you left the shift direction as the default (right-shift). Just to confirm the
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] resulting behavior: with right-shift, your `out pindirs, 3` instructions will consume the lowest 3
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits of your `color_trains` words first, working their way up to bit 17. If you authored your
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] binary literals with the first chronological pulse on the far right, then this is perfectly correct
2026-05-11 21:38:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] and requires no extra configuration.
