2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] How this works:
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. `wait 1 irq 0 clear` stalls the SM until `vidbit` signals the start of the active line.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. `mov y, isr` loads `863` into the loop counter `Y`.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The `pixel` loop consumes 4 bits of data from the TX FIFO.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. The decoding tree routes to `t0`, `t1`, `t2`, or `t3`.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. Every single path takes exactly 10 cycles from `out pins, 2` to `jmp y--, pixel`.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6. When `Y` reaches `-1`, it falls through the bottom, hits `.wrap`, and goes back to waiting for
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] the next IRQ.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] One final check for your C++
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] I noticed you haven't set up a DMA channel to feed pixel data to the `colorpump` SM yet. Because
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] `colorpump` is now executing `out pins, 2` and `out x, 2`, it will immediately stall waiting for
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] data in `pio1`'s TX FIFO.
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] Before you run this, you will need to add a `dmachannelconfigure` to stream your 4-bit
2026-05-11 21:52:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] framebuffer into `pio1` SM 1, or it will never advance past the first pixel!
