2026-05-11 22:05:58 [simon@midnightblue.local] colorpump has new cycle counts
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see what you are trying to do with the `done2` and `nop` padding to balance the cycles. The math
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] for your padding is actually logical, but there is a hardware trap in the RP-series PIO that
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely breaks the `jmp x--` cascade.
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the PIO, `jmp x--` means "Jump if X is not zero, and decrement X in either case."
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because it always decrements X, you cannot chain them together. Here is exactly what the
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] silicon does with your code if the pixel's pump index is `0`:
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. `out x, 2` -> `X` is now `0`.
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. `jmp x--, t3` -> `X` is `0`, so it does not jump. But it decrements `X`. `X` rolls over
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] to `0xFFFFFFFF` (-1).
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. `jmp x--, t2` -> `X` is now `0xFFFFFFFF`. Because `-1` is not zero, it jumps to `t2`.
2026-05-11 22:06:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] As a result, if the color index is `0`, it executes `t2`! If the index is `1`, `2`, or `3`, the
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] very first `jmp x--, t3` evaluates as true, so they all jump to `t3`. The `t0` and `t1` labels are
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] physically unreachable.
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Correct Way to Decode in PIO
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] To decode a value in PIO, you have to use a binary tree of `jmp !x` (which checks for zero without
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] modifying X) and `jmp x--` (to subtract 1 and move to the next check).
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the fully functioning, perfectly cycle-balanced 10-cycle loop. I have calculated the path
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] lengths so every single color takes exactly 10 cycles to execute, keeping your video perfectly in
2026-05-11 22:06:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] phase.
