2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Why 864 is problematic
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Standard PAL (ITU‑R BT.470‑6) defines:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Total line duration: 64.0 µs
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Active video: ≈53.3 µs
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Pixel clock: 13.5 MHz (used in digital video) → 720 active pixels, 864 total samples per
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] line.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Your current setup:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - System clock = 166.5 MHz
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - 10 cycles per pixel → 16.65 MHz pixel clock
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Active time = 864 pixels × 10 cycles = 8640 cycles → 8640 / 166.5 MHz ≈ 51.9 µs (shorter
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] than standard 53.3 µs)
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Total line = 10656 cycles → 64.0 µs (correct)
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Result: non‑standard active sample clock and reduced active period. Most analogue PAL
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] displays may accept this (syncs are timing tolerant), but digital decoders expect 13.5 MHz
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] sampling. Also, 864 active pixels is too many for a typical 720‑pixel‑based video processing
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] chain (e.g., a capture card would scale or crop).
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Recommended change: 12 cycles per pixel at 162 MHz
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] To match standard PAL digital sampling exactly:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ╭─────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Parameter              │  Value                                                                                                            │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Target pixel clock     │  13.5 MHz                                                                                                         │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Cycles per pixel       │  12                                                                                                               │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Required system clock  │  13.5 MHz × 12 = **162 MHz**                                                                                      │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Total line cycles      │  162 MHz × 64 µs = **10368 cycles**                                                                               │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Active cycles          │  13.5 MHz × 53.3 µs = 720 pixels × 12 cycles = **8640 cycles**                                                    │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] │  Sync + porch cycles    │  10368 − 8640 = **1728 cycles** (≈10.6 µs) – matches standard H‑sync + back porch + front porch total of 10.7 µs  │
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ╰─────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] The RP2350 can run at 162 MHz (set `setsysclock_khz(162000, true)`). You will need to
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] recalculate all PIO delays accordingly.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Adjusting the PIO program (`vidbit.pio`)
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] The new timing (at 162 MHz, 12 cycles per pixel, 720 active pixels) requires recalculating the loop
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] counters for HSync, back porch, and front porch. Let me propose a worked example:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - HSync (4.7 µs): 4.7 µs × 162 MHz = 762 cycles → use a loop of `N` iterations with
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] appropriate delays.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Back porch (5.7 µs): 5.7 µs × 162 MHz = 923 cycles.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Active video (53.3 µs): 720 pixels × 12 cycles = 8640 cycles.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Front porch (1.6 µs): 1.6 µs × 162 MHz = 259 cycles.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Total: 762 + 923 + 8640 + 259 = 10584 cycles, but we need 10368. The discrepancy
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] comes from rounding – PAL specs have minor tolerances. You can adjust porch durations to fit
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] exactly.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] • Proposed PIO fragment* (using 12 cycles per pixel):
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ; HSync: 4.7 µs → 762 cycles, e.g. 25 × [30] = 775? Tweak.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set pins, 0
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set pindirs, 1
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set y, 24          ; number of inner loops
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] hsync:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, hsync [31]   ; each iteration = 1 jmp + 31 delay = 32 cycles → 25×32=800? Compute accordingly.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set pindirs, 0
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ; Back porch
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set y, 28
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] backporch:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, backporch [31]   ; 29×32=928? Adjust.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ; Active (720 pixels @12 cycles each = 8640 cycles)
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ; Use a counter that decrements 720 times, each pixel takes 12 cycles via `out pindirs,1 [11]`
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set y, 720
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] active:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4]     out pindirs, 1 [11]   ; 1 out + 11 delay = 12 cycles
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, active
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] ; Front porch
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] set y, 7
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] frontporch:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4]     jmp y--, frontporch [31]   ; 8×32=256 cycles
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] You would need to fine‑tune the counters to hit exact 10368 cycles. A small spreadsheet helps.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Alternative: Keep 166.5 MHz but use 9 cycles per pixel
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] If you prefer to stay at 166.5 MHz:
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - 9 cycles per pixel → 18.5 MHz pixel clock.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Active time: 53.3 µs × 18.5 MHz ≈ 986 pixels (still non‑standard).
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] - Total line: 10656 cycles → 64.0 µs.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] This yields a non‑standard active pixel count of ~986, which is even worse for compatibility. I do
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] not recommend it.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Summary of changes needed
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 1. Change system clock to 162 MHz in `main()`.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 2. Recalculate PIO delays for sync/porch (example above).
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 3. Update pixel counter to 720 (not 864) active pixels.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 4. Adjust other PIO programs (colorfifo, chroma, teletext) to match new pixel clock and
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] line rate.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 5. Update the frame buffer to store 720 pixels per line.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] The 162 MHz clock is a clean multiple of 13.5 MHz and yields integer pixel timing, making the
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] design truly PAL‑compliant.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] Would you like me to provide the exact `setsysclock_khz` call and a revised `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] with correct counters? I can also help recalculate the `threepin.pio` and `colorfifo.pio`
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] pulse‑train lengths accordingly.
2026-05-14 17:45:19 [deepseek-v4] 
