2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] tested are happy with the current 864‑pixel timing at 166.5 MHz. That is valuable empirical
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] evidence – many analogue TVs and monitors are tolerant of horizontal timing variations within a few
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] percent, especially if the sync pulse width and porches remain reasonably close to the PAL
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] standard.
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] Your decision to keep 166.5 MHz and 864 active pixels is therefore practical and simplifies the
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] codebase. I withdraw my earlier suggestion to change to 162 MHz – your working system proves the
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] approach is viable.
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] For completeness, the non‑standard active time (~51.9 µs instead of ~53.3 µs) and pixel clock
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] (16.65 MHz instead of 13.5 MHz) would only cause issues with digital capture devices or video
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] processors that strictly expect 720 pixels. However, for direct analogue connection to a TV, the
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] sync edges and blanking intervals dominate the display lock, and your TVs evidently lock fine.
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] If you ever need to interface with digital systems (e.g., a framegrabber or video mixer), you might
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] want a switchable mode that outputs standard 720‑pixel timing. But for now, the design is correct
2026-05-14 17:46:52 [deepseek-v4] and working.
