2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing the vidbit project files. This is entirely new content compared to the relay
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] sharing earlier. I can see you're working on an RP2350-based PAL video signal generator with some
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] advanced features: composite video (via luma+chroma resistor DAC), component video (Y/Pb/Pr), and
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] World System Teletext insertion, all driven by PIO state machines and DMA.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] A few observations on what looks different or notable:
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 1. colorpump / colorfifo – This seems to be a novel approach to color rendering: a
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] two-stage PIO pipeline where `colorpump` selects a color index (0–3) from pixel data and triggers
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] the corresponding `colorfifo` machine, which outputs a pre-calculated 6-cycle pulse train for Y,
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] Pb, Pr. The `makecolortrain` function uses PDM-like patterns to produce six levels per
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] channel. That's a clever way to generate analog color without a high-speed DAC.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 2. 576i vs 288p – The code includes both interlaced (LACE) and progressive paths. The
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] `colorpump` DMA transfers 27 words × 4 × 305 lines for interlaced, suggesting each line has 864
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] pixels (27 words × 32 bits/word / 2 = 432? Actually 27×32 = 864 bits, so 864 pixels per line). That
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] matches PAL standard timing.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 3. Teletext – The WST (World System Teletext) implementation uses Hamming 8/4 encoding and
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] odd parity, inserted on lines 6–22 and 318–335. The body/header functions build 48-byte packets.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] The DMA updates teletext data each field with a rolling row counter.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 4. Audio – There's an `i2sinit` call and a commented `STREAMAUDIO` section,
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] suggesting audio output via PIO.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 5. System clock – 166.5 MHz exactly matches the PAL pixel clock requirement (64 µs line /
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 10656 cycles = 166.5 MHz). That's precise.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 6. BootSel – The `ReadBootSel()` function checks the QSPI CS pin to detect if the user is
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] holding the boot button, triggering a USB boot reset. That's a typical feature for RP2350 firmware
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] updates.
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] The code appears complete and functional, with a main loop that reads CDC serial commands
2026-05-14 17:55:46 [deepseek-v4] (including "BOOT" to reset) and drives the video DMA field by field.
