2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4]  ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.6 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.016 279.8KB 55.19s
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] I see the issue you're describing—an overrun (likely TX stall/overrun) in progressive mode causing
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] the LED to blink. Based on my review of your code, the problem is almost certainly a mismatch
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] between the data your progressive frame buffer provides and what the PIO program expects.
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] Why the LED blinks
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] In your main loop:
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] if (pio0->fdebug & videoStall) {
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4]     pio0->fdebug = videoStall;
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4]     led = !led;
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] The LED toggles every time the PIO's TX FIFO stalls. A stall on `PULL` or `OUT` (with autopull)
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] happens when the TX FIFO is empty, meaning the DMA is not feeding data fast enough or the data it
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] sends is consumed incorrectly.
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] Given that the DMA is configured for `progressiveWordCount` and `progressiveFrame`, the stall is
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] almost certainly because the progressive frame buffer does not contain the correct preamble
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] that the PIO expects before the per‑line pixel data.
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] What the PIO expects
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] Look at the beginning of `vidbit.pio`:
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; fifo
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; [0] vsync pulsetrain edge-width counter = 32-1
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; [1..32] vsync pulse widths = 1:31 = total 31965 - 2*32
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; [n+1] linecounter = 305/304
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; [n+2] pixelcounter = 863
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] ; [n+3..] bitmap = [27 x 309/310]
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] The very first instruction executed is:
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] out x, 32
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:29 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] This shifts 32 bits from the OSR into X. The OSR must be already loaded via a `PULL` – but there is
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] no explicit `PULL` before that instruction. The autopull must be enabled, or a `PULL` must happen
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] beforehand. In your `vidinit()` you set
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] `smconfigsetout_shift(&config,true,true,32)` which enables autopull with a
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] threshold of 32. Therefore, the first `out x, 32` will automatically trigger a `PULL` from the TX
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] FIFO to fill the OSR, then shift those 32 bits into X. So the first word the DMA sends must
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] be the vsync pulse train edge‑width counter (typically 31 for 32 pulses).
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] After that, the program executes a loop of `pulse:` – `out y, 31` → `jmp y--, phase` → `jmp x--,
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] pulse`. Each iteration consumes two words from the FIFO (one for the `out y, 31` and one for the
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] `out x, 32`? Actually careful: `out y, 31` shifts from OSR, then `jmp y--, phase` – delay inserts,
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] but no more pulls. The loop repeats 32 times? X starts with the first word (32‑1 = 31) and is
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] decremented each iteration. So the next words in the FIFO are the 32 pulse widths (each 31 bits of
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] delay? Actually `out y, 31` expects another word because OSR will be consumed. But autopull will
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] refill OSR after each OUT if the shift count reaches the threshold (32). So after the first `out x,
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] 32`, the OSR is empty and autopull triggers on the next OUT. That means the FIFO must be sequenced
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] as:
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] - Word 0: vsync pulse count (31)
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] - Words 1..32: 32 pulse widths (each 32‑bit word, but only lower 31 bits used for `out y, 31`)
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] Then after the vsync, the program does:
2026-05-18 14:36:30 [deepseek-v4] 
