2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for sharing the files. This is an ambitious project — generating PAL 576i video from a
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] Pico2 at 166.5 MHz with a combination of PIO state machines and a passive resistor‑DAC network.
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] I’ll offer comments on the architecture, PIO code, timing, and potential pitfalls.
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] Overall architecture
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] You describe a pipeline of three state machines:
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] - pixelpump – reads two colour‑palette bit‑trains from the FIFO, then pumps 27×32‑bit words
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] (864 pixels) per line.
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] - onebit – takes the 32‑bit pixel word and outputs 1‑bit at a time, selecting either the
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] `x` or `y` waveform (colour 0/1) based on the pixel bit.
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] - threepin – for each pixel, drives 3 tristate pins with 10 cycles of tri‑state data
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] followed by 2 cycle gap (alpha). This is the final analogue output stage.
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-19 18:54:06 [deepseek-v4] Then you have the central vidbit.pio which generates the full PAL line timing (HSYNC, back
