2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] I've reviewed the provided codebase for "vidbit" – a PAL video signal generator for the RP2350
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] (Pico 2) using PIO and resistor-based DACs. The architecture is ambitious: multi‑lane component
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] video, teletext, chroma burst, audio I²S, PS/2 input, and a little‑fs filesystem, all driven by PIO
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] state machines and DMA. Below I offer a structured analysis, focusing on correctness, timing, and
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] maintainability.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] Overall Strengths
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Good separation of concerns: Each PIO program (`vidbit.pio`, `chroma.pio`, `onebit.pio`,
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] etc.) handles a specific part of the pipeline. The C++ code organises initialisation and rendering
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] logically.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Use of RP2350‑specific features: Indexed RX FIFO (FJOIN_RX_GET/PUT) for lookup tables,
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] cross‑PIO IRQ synchronisation, and multiple PIO blocks are employed purposefully.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Frame buffer design: Interlaced (576i) and progressive (288p) modes are supported with
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] separate even/odd frame buffers, inline assembly‑like pixel drawing, and DMA‑driven transfer.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Teletext (WST) implementation: Hamming 8/4 encoding and odd‑parity generation are
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] correctly implemented; the packet structure matches the specification.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] Potential Issues & Suggestions
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 1. PAL Timing Constraints in `vidbit.pio`
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] The line timing totals 10656 cycles at 166.5 MHz (64 µs). However, some constant calculations
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] appear approximate:
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] ; hsync = ~4.71 µs = 786 cycles (27*29+3)
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] set y, HSYNC_WAIT29	;26
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] hsync:
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] jmp y--,hsync [28]
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - `HSYNC_WAIT29` is defined as 26, but the loop runs `y`+1 times (due to `jmp y--`). With delay 28
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] per iteration, total cycles = `(26+1)(1+28) = 2729 = 783` plus initial `set` (1) and final
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] `jmp exit`? Actually the loop includes the `set` and `jmp`, so the count may be off by a few
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] cycles. Recommend calculating exact cycle counts for each region (HSYNC, back porch, active, front
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] porch) and using `.define` arithmetic to avoid drift.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Similarly, `BACKPORCH_WAIT32` (26) with delay 31 gives `(26+1)*32 = 864` cycles, but comment says
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 964. Off by 100.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] • Risk*: Over time, line length drifts; after many lines, vertical sync may shift. Use a
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] single counter for the whole line or precise cycle‑counting.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 2. Interlaced Field Synchronisation
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] In `pal576i.cpp`, the preamble for each field (even/odd) includes equalising and broad sync pulses,
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] but the order of half‑lines may not match the PAL specification for 625/50i.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - The odd field should start with a half‑line of black (S0/S1) then equalising pulses, but the code
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] starts with `S0,S1` for the even field and `E0,E1,...` for the odd field. Check that the first line
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] of each field begins at the correct phase (e.g., odd field starts with a full line of video after
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] the half‑line offset).
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - The constant `VSYNCSIMPLE` (2×2661) is used in `progressiveFrame` but then commented out. For
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] progressive, there’s no need for equalising or broad pulses; just a vertical sync period. The
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] current preamble appears overcomplicated for 288p.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 3. Pixel Pipeline: `onebit.pio` and `threepin.pio`
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] The pipeline `pixelpump -> onebit -> threepin` is intended to produce three‑lane output (Y, Pb,
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] Pr). However:
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - `onebit.pio` uses a loopback pin (GPIO 46) to read pixel state, but the `jmp pin` condition reads
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] from the JMP pin, not the loopback. The loopback is configured as an OUT pin? It’s unclear if the
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] hardware loopback works as intended.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - `threepin.pio` shifts out 30 bits per pixel (3 lanes × 10 bits), then skips 2 bits “alpha”. At 10
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] cycles per pixel, that’s 3×10 = 30 cycles if done serially? Actually it outputs 3 bits per cycle
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] via `out pindirs, 3` repeated 10 times, which is 30 cycles. But the pixel period is 10 cycles, so
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] this SM runs at 3× pixel clock. The comment says “10 cycles per pixel” – that may be the system
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] clock ratio, not the SM speed. Need to ensure clock dividers match.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] • Suggestion*: Simulate the PIO programs with a cycle‑accurate emulator (e.g., `picotool`
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] or hardware in the loop) to validate timing before fabrication.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 4. Chroma Subcarrier Generation
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] The `chroma.pio` program drives two pins (CHROMA100, CHROMA330) at 17.73 MHz (4× subcarrier). The
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] divider is set to 2.3471 (≈166.5/17.734475). However, the PIO fractional divider may introduce
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] jitter; a better approach is to use a dedicated state machine with a precise integer divider and
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] use a PLL output if available (RP2350 has no video PLL, but fractional divider delta‑sigma may be
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] acceptable for composite video). The burst pattern in `initChroma()` uses a 2‑bit sine
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] approximation – that will work but may cause colour desaturation or ringing. Consider using a
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] higher‑resolution sinusoidal table (e.g., 4‑bit) and an R‑2R ladder.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 5. DMA Interlocking
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] The main loop polls `dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)` before starting the next field. This is
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] fine, but if the DMA completes while software is still rendering, the next field will be delayed by
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] the rendering time. For 25 Hz fields, rendering must finish within 40 ms. The drawing code in
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] `drawFramei2()` uses floating‑point `sin` and loops; on a 166 MHz dual‑core, it may be tight.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] Profiling recommended.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Idle cores: Another core could handle rendering while the first manages DMA and USB. The
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] `multicore` SDK could be used.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 6. Peripheral Conflicts
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - `ps2_init` uses GPIO 0,1,2,3 for PS/2 data and clock. Meanwhile `spdif_init` uses GPIO 18 (SPDIF
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] input), and `i2s_init` uses GPIOs 2,3,4 for audio. Overlap on GPIO 2 and 3 between PS/2 and I²S –
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] this will cause contention. Fix: Assign unique pins.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - `colorpump_init` in `colorbit.cpp` maps `COLOR_I0` and `COLOR_I1` to GPIO 0 and 1, again
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] conflicting with PS/2. Unify pin assignments in a single header.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 7. Code Quality
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Many preprocessor conditionals (`#ifdef COLOR`, `#ifdef GREY`, etc.) make the code hard to
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] follow. Consider converting to build‑time configuration via CMake targets.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - Global variables (e.g., `snow`, `led`) are scattered. Use classes or namespaces for state.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] - The `json.h` and `events.h` headers are large and include `ioqspi.h` etc. – they seem unrelated
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] to the video core. Extract them into a separate library to reduce compilation footprint.
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] 8. Teletext – Line Count Mismatch
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] `wstRow` is incremented inside both `updateTeletextEven` and `updateTeletextOdd`, but they are
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] called once per field each. That means `wstRow` advances two rows per frame (one per field). The
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] teletext rows are per field (lines 7–22 in each field), so row should reset per field, not
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] per frame. Also, the maximum row is 24, but you increment without wrap at 25 then reset to 1 –
2026-05-22 21:04:09 [deepseek-v4] that’s fine.
