2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] Better approach: use the clock divider
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] We can keep your original two‑loop structure (the one that gave ~19.7 kHz) and divide the state
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] machine clock to bring the frequency down to 440 Hz. The original program produced ~19.7 kHz;
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] to get 440 Hz we need a divider of 19 700 / 440 ≈ 44.8. Since the divisor is 16.8 fixed‑point, we
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] can set `clkdiv = 45.0` (or 44.75 with fractional part). That gives exact 440 Hz with negligible
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] error.
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] Revised `spkr.pio` (unchanged program – just add clock divider in C)
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] ; spkr.pio – unchanged from your original idea
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] .program spkr
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     pins, 1
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     x, 3
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] loop1:
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     y, 31
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] loop2:
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     nop     [31]
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     y--, loop2
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     x--, loop1
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     pins, 0
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     x, 3
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] loop3:
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     set     y, 31
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] loop4:
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     nop     [31]
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     y--, loop4
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     jmp     x--, loop3
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] .wrap
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] In C, initialise the state machine with a clock divider:
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] void spkr_init(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &spkr_program);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     pio_sm_config c = spkr_program_get_default_config(offset);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     sm_config_set_out_pins(&c, SPKR_PIN, 1);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     // set clkdiv so that SM runs at 166.5 MHz / 44.75 ≈ 3.72 MHz
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     // Original program period = 8468 SM cycles → 3.72e6 / 8468 ≈ 439.5 Hz
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     sm_config_set_clkdiv(&c, 44.75f);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] }
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] This gives ~439.5 Hz – imperceptibly flat.
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] If you want exactly 440.0 Hz, you can compute a precise fractional divider. Here the
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] required divider is `166.5e6 / (2  8468  440) ≈ 44.72`. Use `sm_config_set_clkdiv(&c,
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 166.5e6 / (2  8468  440.0f));`.
2026-05-22 21:13:32 [deepseek-v4] 
